Epistaxis (Severe)
Acute Urgent Care
Profuse active nasal bleeding, Posterior pharyngeal blood flow, Failure of direct pressure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-031
Reason for Visit: Unstoppable, heavy nosebleed lasting more than 45 minutes, causing the patient to swallow large amounts of blood and feel faint.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported continuous, profuse bleeding from the nose that failed to arrest after 30 minutes of direct pressure, accompanied by the patient coughing up large blood clots. This presentation signifies a high risk of airway compromise from posterior pharyngeal blood flow and potential hypovolaemic shock. Urgent, face-to-face assessment and intervention with chemical cautery or nasal packing were required, as waiting for a routine appointment would lead to significant blood loss and aspiration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I've been pinching my nose for almost an hour, but the blood just keeps pouring down the back of my throat and making me sick.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with continuous, profuse active bleeding from the anterior and posterior nasal cavity that began spontaneously 1 hour ago. The patient reports frequent swallowing of blood, which has led to one episode of haematemesis (vomiting swallowed blood). There is a constant sensation of blood flowing down the back of the throat. The spouse notes the patient has been expectorating large blood clots from the posterior pharynx into a basin. The bleeding has completely failed to arrest after 45 minutes of direct, continuous pressure applied to the soft part of the nose. The patient feels increasingly lightheaded.
Functional Status: Sitting forward on a chair, holding a blood-soaked towel to the face.
Social/Safety Concerns: Aspiration risk; significant volume loss.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is pale, mildly diaphoretic, and visibly anxious, with a continuous trickle of dark blood from the right naris.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy (after asking the patient to gently blow out clots) reveals a prominent, actively bleeding arterial vessel on the anterior right nasal septum (Kiesselbach's plexus). Oropharyngeal examination reveals active posterior pharyngeal blood flow. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates resting tachycardia and borderline hypotension, indicating early volume depletion. Expected investigation results in a hospital setting would show an isolated acute drop in serum haemoglobin on a complete blood count.
Environmental Observations: Multiple blood-soaked towels and a basin of clotted blood on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient takes daily Aspirin 100mg and Ramipril 10mg.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Epistaxis (Severe) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Coagulopathy (e.g., undiagnosed Hemophilia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of spontaneous bruising elsewhere or a prior history of bleeding disorders, though the daily aspirin use is a significant contributing factor.

Nasal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clear visualization of a superficial bleeding vessel on the septum rather than a friable fungating mass, though outpatient ENT follow-up is prudent.

Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed - Ruled out/unlikely due to the witnessed primary source of bleeding being the nasal cavity, with the haematemesis clearly secondary to swallowed blood.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the diagnostic tetrad of severe epistaxis: profuse active nasal bleeding, posterior pharyngeal blood flow, and failure of direct pressure. The objective signs of tachycardia and pallor indicate significant ongoing blood loss. The urgent home visit was essential because conservative first-aid measures had failed. Without immediate clinical intervention (such as the application of topical vasoconstrictors and nasal packing) to mechanically tamponade the bleeding vessel, the patient was at high risk of airway aspiration and progression to hemorrhagic shock before daytime medical services opened.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleared the nasal cavity of clots. Applied topical Co-phenylcaine spray (vasoconstrictor/anesthetic). As bleeding continued, successfully inserted a Rapid Rhino (anterior/posterior inflatable nasal tampon) into the right nostril and inflated it with air. Bleeding arrested within 3 minutes.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly avoiding any nose-picking, heavy lifting, or hot showers for the next 72 hours, you prevent the fragile, newly formed blood clot from dislodging and triggering another massive bleed.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Proceed to the local Emergency Department for ongoing observation and safe removal of the nasal pack in 24-48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the bleeding starts bypassing the balloon packing and pouring down your throat again, or if you feel severe dizziness or chest pain, call 000 immediately for an ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The balloon inside your nose is a temporary measure to physically crush the bleeding blood vessel until it clots securely.
Clinical Course: The packing must remain in place for 24 to 48 hours and should only be deflated and removed by a doctor in the hospital to ensure the vessel does not tear open again.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to pull the balloon out yourself under any circumstances. Keep your head elevated above your heart at all times, even when sleeping, to reduce the blood pressure in your nose.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have inserted a specialized balloon that puts direct, high-pressure force against the bleeding artery deep inside your nose. Your daily aspirin made it impossible for your body to form a strong clot on its own.
Actionable Steps: Have your spouse drive you to the Emergency Department now so they can admit you for observation overnight. Breathe strictly through your mouth and spit out any saliva rather than swallowing it to avoid upsetting your stomach.

